    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65308",
      "knowledge_point_id": "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326",
      "knowledge_point": "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is all real numbers.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$\n- $x=5\\pi $ is in the domain of the function.",
      "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940224/assets/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi ,$ so $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$ Substituing $n=1,$ we get $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$ Hence, Statement II is true.\n\n- The function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is defined for all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi ,$ so $5\\pi $ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is false.",
