    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-103822",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6,$ then what is $\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=8$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 8\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )=6.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+16\\pi )+5 & =\\frac{1}{3}(6)+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3}+5 \\\\  & =2+5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "10"
            },
