      "math_academy_id": "q-103820",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{5},$ then what is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi )?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{5},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+14\\pi )=\\sqrt{5}.$",
