    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-66605",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2",
      "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2,$ then what is $\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )+2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2,$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=2.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )+2 & =\\frac{1}{4}(2)+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-\\frac{1}{4}+\\pi "
